CR-03 validation round: adversarial probing found the verb-gating incomplete; now fixed
(commit fd38d77), all gates green.

## Why this round happened
An independent validation pass was requested rather than trusting the earlier report. It
did not take the previous fix on faith: it read the committed regex, then ran an
adversarial probe (a throwaway vitest file, deleted afterwards) through the real
`hasCraftEditIntent` module with cases the committed suite did not cover.

## What the probe found
**The verb-gating was incomplete.** Eight mismatches against correct behaviour:

1. Seven FALSE POSITIVES, all the same shape: a verb that is *also* a noun, used as the
   object of a question. "what is the export format?", "what is the blur radius?",
   "what is the zoom level?", "what is the print size?", "what is the scale of the map?",
   "what does polish mean?" and the Arabic "ما هي صيغة تصدير الملف؟", "ما هو تصميم
   الشعار؟", "ما هو دمج الملفين؟", "ما هو تقسيم الصفحات؟", "ما هو التلوين المناسب؟".
   These ask about a term and still auto-opened the Craft editor.
2. One FALSE NEGATIVE: "make the whole background slightly darker" (three words between
   `make` and the comparative, beyond the two the pattern allowed).

The root cause is that a word list alone cannot separate the verb from the noun; the
distinguishing signal is the question frame around it.

## What changed in `autoOpen.ts`
- `QUESTION_FRAME_GUARDS`: a set of fixed-width lookbehinds placed in front of the whole
  alternation, so they are evaluated at whichever position the match starts. They reject
  a verb that is the object of `what is the` / `what's the` / `what does` / `what are the`
  and the Arabic `ما هو ` / `ما هي `, plus `صيغة `, `شكل `, `نوع ` and `طريقة ` (format,
  shape, type, method), which is exactly where these nouns appear.
- Added the Arabic imperative `صدّر`/`صدر` (the list carried only the verbal noun
  تصدير), so "صدّر الملف إلى pdf" matches.
- Widened the `make ... comparative` window from 2 to 4 intervening words.
- No English verb was removed, so every previously-working edit request keeps working.

## Verification (not assumed)
- Probe against the real module: 28 innocent cases (0 mismatches) and 37 edit cases
  (0 mismatches), including the Arabic noun/verb frames, capitalisation variants
  ("What is the export format?") and punctuation.
- ReDoS sanity: a 40 KB adversarial string returns in 9 ms (no catastrophic
  backtracking; all constructs are linear/atomic).
- The committed suite now pins this: `craftAutoOpen.test.ts` grew from 18 to 20 tests,
  adding 11 guard cases and 8 real-verb cases (Arabic included). The two added tests are
  the ones the probe validated. No test was weakened or skipped.

## Gates (run from the worktree root on the committed tree, fd38d77)
- `npx tsc --noEmit` (root)                        -> exit 0
- `(cd packages/web && npx tsc --noEmit)`          -> exit 0
- `(cd packages/web && npx vitest run)`           -> 75 files / 506 tests, all pass
- `npm run build:server`                           -> exit 0 (pdf worker copied)
- `(cd packages/web && npx next build)`           -> exit 0 (compiled, 25/25 static pages)
- `npm run check:encoding`                         -> OK (no BOM, no mojibake, all JSON valid)

Working tree clean; the probe file and the temporary fix attempts were deleted before
committing. Note for reviewers: `edit` silently unescapes `\\b`/`\\s` in TS regex literals
in this environment, which broke the module mid-edit; the fix was applied with
`apply_patch` and the regex bytes were re-verified against HEAD.

